From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 15:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/h264dec: keep the tables across a flush

A flush freed every table and marked the context uninitialised, so the
first slice after a seek rebuilt all of them, the per-picture pools
included: at 3840x2160 about 5 MB a picture, zeroed again. A stream that
goes on with the same SPS needs none of it, and one that changes it is
reinitialised by h264_init_ps(), whose h264_slice_header_init() frees
the old tables first.

On an RK3528 decoding 4K H.264 through V4L2 requests, a three-frame clip
looped 200 times took 12.2 s and 3.5 s of CPU; the same 600 frames with
no flush took 10.3 s and 1.0 s.
---
diff --git a/libavcodec/h264dec.c b/libavcodec/h264dec.c
index a88ce95..a735b3e 100644
--- a/libavcodec/h264dec.c
+++ b/libavcodec/h264dec.c
@@ -491,9 +491,6 @@ static av_cold void h264_decode_flush(AVCodecContext *avctx)
     h->mb_y = 0;
     h->non_gray = 0;
 
-    ff_h264_free_tables(h);
-    h->context_initialized = 0;
-
     if (FF_HW_HAS_CB(avctx, flush))
         FF_HW_SIMPLE_CALL(avctx, flush);
 }
